TEST: wifi: ath11k: build extension descriptor frames as the Android host does

Test only. A batch sent through MSDU extension descriptors makes the
firmware fail, and so does a scatter-gather frame. The host driver that
Android builds for the same TCL (qca-wifi-host-cmn, used with TSO on
QCA6390 and QCA6490 radios) differs from ours in ways that did not matter
to the hardware headers, and one of them it carries under a build option
named for a Wi-Fi TX DMA limitation, which it enables on its QCA6490 and
QCA6750 configurations.

With module parameter tso_android (default on, read for each frame except
where stated):

- The buffer that ends the headers ends on an 8 byte boundary. For a TCP
  batch that is the headers, for a scatter-gather frame the linear part. The
  host driver slides the headers down by the excess in the head room of the
  frame, moving the header offsets with them, and maps the payload where it
  was. The frame needs 8 bytes of head room and an unshared head
  (skb_cow_head()); the slide is undone if the frame is not queued.
- A descriptor never straddles a 4096 byte boundary of the descriptor memory,
  as the descriptor pool of the vendor driver, which is made of pages. The
  slots are placed per block; read when the rings are allocated.
- The IP, UDP and TCP checksum enables of the TCL command are all set for a
  batch, as the vendor driver sets them, and the TCP NS flag of its segments
  is cleared, which that driver never sends.

With the parameter off the frames are built as before.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
